                "text": "Course Description",
                "category": "title_candidate",
                "reason": "First text element of the slide"
            },
            {
                "element_id": 2,
                "text": "CS106AX: Programming Methodologies in JavaScript and Python\n\nIntroduction to the engineering of computer and web applications emphasizing modern software engineering principles: object-oriented design, decomposition, encapsulation, abstraction, and testing. This course targets an audience with prior programming experience, and that prior experience is leveraged so material can be covered in greater depth.\n\nTerms: Aut | Units: 3-5 | UG Reqs: WAY-FR | Grading: Letter or CR/NC",
                "category": "paragraph_candidate",
                "reason": "General explanatory text"
            },
            {
                "element_id": 3,
                "text": "CS106AX satisfies the same WAYS requirements as any other CS106A offering.",
                "category": "paragraph_candidate",
                "reason": "General explanatory text"
            }
        ]
    },
    {
        "slide_number": 3,
        "content": [
            {
                "element_id": 1,
                "text": "Why JavaScript?",
                "category": "title_candidate",
                "reason": "First text element of the slide"
            },
            {
                "element_id": 2,
                "text": "When CS106A adopted Java some 20 years ago, we—along with many others in the computing industry—expected it to become the \"language of the web\".",
                "category": "paragraph_candidate",
                "reason": "General explanatory text"
            },
            {
                "element_id": 3,
                "text": "That didn’t happen.",
                "category": "paragraph_candidate",
                "reason": "General explanatory text"
            },
            {
                "element_id": 4,
                "text": "Today, the core \"language of the web\" is JavaScript, which has become one of the most widely used languages in industry.\nJavaScript expert Douglas Crockford has described JavaScript—provided you avoid some of its most frequently abused features—as \"a beautiful, elegant, highly expressive language\". I believe it’s also ideal for a first course in programming.\nIt is, in my opinion, syntactically easier to learn than Java.\nThere are far fewer details to memorize.\nIt offers cleaner implementations of many key language features.\nIt is natively supported by every major web browser.",
                "category": "paragraph_candidate",
                "reason": "Multiple complete explanatory sentences detected"
            },
            {
                "element_id": 5,
                "text": "😢",
                "category": "other_candidate",
                "reason": "No meaningful alphanumeric content"
            }
        ]
    },
